WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.010
Our vehicle is constructed
from foam with carbon fiber

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:08.340
reinforced wings.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.320
It has a single control
surface at the tail,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.530
which is controlled by
a hobby servo motor.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:15.140
For our experiment, we will
only consider the aircraft's

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.270
lateral planar dynamics.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:18.860
For tracking the
aircraft's position,

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.411
we'll use a VICON
motion capture studio.

00:00:21.411 --> 00:00:22.910
We will define our
perching maneuver

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:24.950
as the aircraft
starting 3.5 meters

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.380
from the perch at 7 meters
per second and landing

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.600
in a 6.5 centimeter
capture region

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.010
with less than 1 meter per
second of forward velocity.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.840
We demonstrate the
robustness of our system

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.160
by throwing the glider by hand.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.570
Landing on power
lines to recharge

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:04.480
provides a unique opportunity
to improve the mission duration

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.280
of unmanned aerial vehicles.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.680
In this project, we
explore whether or not

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:10.720
we can successfully
perch on a power line

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.620
with a fixed wing UAV using only
the magnetic field generated

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:16.810
by the power line to
localize the aircraft.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.250
These results demonstrate that
magnetic field based estimates

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.470
are sufficient for achieving
closed-loop perching

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:30.810
and could be a reliable
means of perching

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.830
on powerlines in the field.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:36.060
Finally, we take our power
line perching glider outdoors

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.400
and demonstrate that we are
able to perch successfully

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.870
in outdoor environments
when wind is limited.

